'''willowing-machine''' (wil′ọ̄-ing-mạ-shēn″), ''n.'' Same as ''willow''<sup>2</sup>.

'''willowish''' (wil′ọ̄-ish), ''a.'' [< ''willow''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ish''<sup>1</sup>.] Resembling the willow; like the color of the willow. ''I. Walton'', Complete Angler, i. 5.

'''willow-lark''' (wil′ō-lärk), ''n.'' The sedge-warbler. ''Pennant'', 1768. (''Imp. Dict.'')

'''willow-leaf''' (wil′ō-lēf), ''n.'' One of the elongated filaments of which the solar photosphere appears to be composed, especially in the neighborhood of sun-spots. {{smaller|The name was proposed by Nasmyth, but is no longer in general use, since as a rule the photospheric granules are not of a form to justify it.}}

'''willow-machine''' (wil′ō-mạ-shēn″), ''n.'' Same as ''willow''<sup>2</sup>.

'''willow-moth''' (wil′ō-môth), ''n.'' A common British noctuid moth, ''Caradrina quadripunctata'', a pale mottled species whose caterpillar does much damage to stored grain.

'''willow-myrtle''' (wil′ō-mėr″tl), ''n.'' A myrtaceous tree with willow-like leaves, ''Agonis flexuosa'', of western Australia, growing 40 feet high.

